Description: antinomie: hide the recorder from programs in the guest
 A sample that looks for a sandbox reads world-readable files. Remove
 what the recorder itself put there.
 .
 - Boot options: the host passes antinomie_boot_gpa= and
   antinomie_ring_kib= on the command line. setup_arch() now cuts every
   antinomie_* option out of the line before it is first printed and
   applies it as an early parameter, so /proc/cmdline and the kernel log
   never show it. The decompressor still reads the original line.
 - Symbols: /proc/kallsyms lists every kernel symbol by name to any user,
   and /proc/<pid>/wchan names the function a task sleeps in.
   section_begin.o and section_end.o, linked first and last in
   kernel/antinomie, bracket the recorder's sections, and
   ant_symbol_hidden() answers for those ranges and for the hooks'
   names elsewhere; both files skip them. The bootstrap's early parameter
   handler is renamed after the recorder for the same reason. The
   kernel's own symbol table stays complete for stack traces, kprobes
   and the debugger.
 - Worker names: a kworker shows the workqueue it last served. The
   control, process core and debug queues are named events_unbound like
   the system queue, and the resource refresh runs on the system queue.
 - ACPI: QEMU's own devices (_HID QEMU*, such as fw_cfg) are not
   enumerated, so sysfs and /proc/ioports do not name QEMU.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - arch/x86/kernel/setup.c: the command line, before its first print.
 - kernel/kallsyms.c and fs/proc/base.c: the /proc/kallsyms listing and
   wchan.
 - drivers/acpi/scan.c: the device scan.
 .
 Root can still find the recorder (/proc/kcore, a kprobe by name);
 that is inspecting the kernel, not reading a listing. The host passes
 the same options as before.
 .
 Contract: unchanged (revision 17).
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
